1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,380
want to consume more than exists.

2
00:00:01,380 --> 00:00:02,560
You can get the doubt that they will consume it,

3
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:04,380
that they want to consume more than exists.

4
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:07,380
So there's got to be a price so astronomical that people say,

5
00:00:07,380 --> 00:00:10,380
fine, we're going to consume less than actually exists.

6
00:00:10,380 --> 00:00:12,100
There's got to be a price low enough

7
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:14,420
that people will say we want to consume more than actually

8
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:15,780
exists.

9
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:18,260
And then last, we just need the total demand function for k.

10
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:23,340
Is it got to be continuous for all prices between 0 and 1?

11
00:00:23,340 --> 00:00:26,020
And now, amazingly, we're done.

12
00:00:26,020 --> 00:00:28,540
So how do we actually prove the theorem?

13
00:00:28,540 --> 00:00:30,220
Well, there's the special case.

14
00:00:30,220 --> 00:00:33,460
If it is a two commodity world, then there

15
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:35,100
is a theorem you may actually remember

16
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:37,940
from calculus called the intermediate value theorem.

17
00:00:37,940 --> 00:00:40,100
This is basically like a Goldilocks theorem

18
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:43,180
where it says, look, if sometimes it's too hot

19
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:44,700
and sometimes it's too cold, there

20
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:47,980
must be a point at which it is just right.

21
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:51,140
And that's if there's a continuum of porridge

22
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:54,940
temperatures, then there must be a bowl of porridge

23
00:00:54,940 --> 00:00:57,020
at a temperature that is exactly right.

24
00:00:57,020 --> 00:00:59,180
So that is the intermediate value theorem.

25
00:00:59,180 --> 00:01:01,740
So if it can be too high, too low, it's continuous,

26
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:05,260
then you can prove that there must be a point where

27
00:01:05,260 --> 00:01:08,420
you cross whatever threshold that you specifically

28
00:01:08,420 --> 00:01:10,820
are concerned about.

29
00:01:10,820 --> 00:01:13,180
Now, what about the k commodity world,

30
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:15,220
just an arbitrary number?

31
00:01:15,220 --> 00:01:18,340
Well, here there is something in topology called Brouwer's fixed

32
00:01:18,340 --> 00:01:20,020
point theorem.

33
00:01:20,020 --> 00:01:22,460
Brouwer's fixed point theorem.

34
00:01:22,460 --> 00:01:24,380
And we're not going to go into what it is,

35
00:01:24,380 --> 00:01:26,380
but you invoke this theorem.

36
00:01:26,380 --> 00:01:28,940
And we have actually used all the assumptions

37
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:33,300
that we need to shoehorn the economy into this famous theorem

38
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:34,660
in topology.

39
00:01:34,660 --> 00:01:39,500
Now, I tell you, I actually got to watch Gerard Dubrow, who

40
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:42,020
is, together with Arrow, proved the existence

41
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:44,500
of general equilibrium using Brouwer's fixed point theorem.

42
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:46,340
So I actually got to be in a class with him

43
00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:48,820
where he went and did his own proof.

44
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:51,540
And it went on for 90 minutes.

45
00:01:51,540 --> 00:01:54,260
And he was the famous Nobel Prize winner.

46
00:01:54,260 --> 00:01:55,820
And then after he finished with it,

47
00:01:55,820 --> 00:01:58,820
he was so proud of himself, as proud as only a Frenchman

48
00:01:58,820 --> 00:01:59,700
could be of himself.

49
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:02,140
And then he finished with it.

50
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:05,980
And then he finished with it.

51
00:02:05,980 --> 00:02:08,300
And my aunt shot up and said, well,

52
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:11,060
but didn't you just assume that the economy fits

53
00:02:11,060 --> 00:02:12,700
the assumptions of another theorem

54
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:13,700
proved by somebody else?

55
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:15,340
What's so great about that?

56
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:17,180
Oh, I did something much better than that.

57
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:30,820
So he never convinced me he had done anything that much better

58
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:31,340
than that.

59
00:02:31,340 --> 00:02:36,980
And I still stick to my view that that was a bad Nobel.

60
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:39,580
But obviously, I'm in the minority here.

61
00:02:39,580 --> 00:02:41,900
And a lot of people thought this was freaking brilliant.

62
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:45,820
So I saw him walk through his own theorem.

63
00:02:45,820 --> 00:02:47,820
And he said, here's Brown's fixed point theorem.

64
00:02:47,820 --> 00:02:48,580
Here's what I did.

65
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:50,300
And they're like, what you're doing

66
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:53,060
is just making assumptions until it works.

67
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:55,100
It's all reverse engineering.

68
00:02:55,100 --> 00:02:57,100
I said, no.

69
00:02:57,100 --> 00:02:59,980
It's much deeper.

70
00:02:59,980 --> 00:03:01,620
All right.

71
00:03:01,620 --> 00:03:04,620
My take is I was right then.

72
00:03:04,620 --> 00:03:05,940
I'm still right now.

73
00:03:05,940 --> 00:03:09,220
And yes, he did something.

74
00:03:09,220 --> 00:03:11,940
But it's not really that great.

75
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:15,300
It's a sad world where that's worth a Nobel Prize.

76
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:16,740
Anyway.

77
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:19,420
But just to give you intuition, so what does Brouwer's fixed

78
00:03:19,420 --> 00:03:20,140
point theorem say?

79
00:03:20,140 --> 00:03:22,180
Or what does any fixed point theorem say?

80
00:03:22,180 --> 00:03:22,780
All right.

81
00:03:22,780 --> 00:03:24,660
So mathematically, the idea is you

82
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:28,380
want to prove conditions under which there is some f of x

83
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:30,340
that is equal to x.

84
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:32,540
Some f of x that is equal to x.

85
00:03:32,540 --> 00:03:34,740
Now intuitively, think about this.

86
00:03:34,740 --> 00:03:36,540
Suppose you look at a globe.

87
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:43,100
And I were to say, show me where on this globe the globe is.

88
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:45,340
And you would go and take your little pen and say,

89
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:46,860
it's right there in Fairfax.

90
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:50,900
The globe is right there on the globe.

91
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:51,740
Or it could be a map.

92
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:56,780
And you could say, all right, where on this map are we,

93
00:03:56,780 --> 00:03:58,740
the people holding the map?

94
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:00,220
And again, you could say, all right,

95
00:04:00,220 --> 00:04:02,380
we're right there, right there.

96
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:04,940
Now first, you might say, well, isn't there always

97
00:04:04,940 --> 00:04:06,860
a place on the globe where the globe is,

98
00:04:06,860 --> 00:04:08,480
or a place on the map where the map is?

99
00:04:08,480 --> 00:04:09,460
And the answer is no.

100
00:04:09,460 --> 00:04:11,980
Suppose that we were on the moon holding

101
00:04:11,980 --> 00:04:13,260
the globe of the Earth.

102
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:16,860
And you say, where on the surface of this globe are we?

103
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:18,700
We're not there.

104
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:20,180
We're in a totally different place.

105
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:23,100
There's no place on this globe of Earth

106
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:26,380
where we moonwalkers are.

107
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:28,900
And that is a case where there would not be a fixed point.

108
00:04:28,900 --> 00:04:31,620
So a fixed point would be if you could show where

109
00:04:31,620 --> 00:04:34,740
the map is on the map itself, or where the globe is positioned

110
00:04:34,740 --> 00:04:36,540
on the globe itself.

111
00:04:36,540 --> 00:04:38,060
And the nonexistence of a fixed point

112
00:04:38,060 --> 00:04:41,420
would be one where you are on the surface of the moon

113
00:04:41,420 --> 00:04:42,460
with a globe of Earth.

114
00:04:42,460 --> 00:04:43,740
And you're asked where you are.

115
00:04:43,740 --> 00:04:45,540
And usually you say, it doesn't exist here.

116
00:04:45,540 --> 00:04:47,340
There is no such place here.

117
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:49,820
And of course, if you are in the United States looking

118
00:04:49,820 --> 00:04:52,300
at a map of Europe, where's America on this map?

119
00:04:52,300 --> 00:04:53,500
America's not on this map.

120
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:55,300
Not anywhere.

121
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:58,180
So that is the idea of a fixed point.

122
00:04:58,180 --> 00:04:59,860
Another analogy that's often used

123
00:04:59,860 --> 00:05:03,060
is suppose there's a mountain with a path.

124
00:05:03,060 --> 00:05:05,980
It's winding, but it never actually reverses.

125
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:08,220
So it always is going down.

126
00:05:08,220 --> 00:05:10,020
Or of course, from the bottom point of view,

127
00:05:10,020 --> 00:05:10,940
it's always going up.

128
00:05:10,940 --> 00:05:12,380
And one person starts at the top.

129
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:13,940
One person starts at the bottom.

130
00:05:13,940 --> 00:05:16,740
Can we be sure that they will meet each other?

131
00:05:16,740 --> 00:05:18,700
And if we've set it up right, then there

132
00:05:18,700 --> 00:05:21,300
is a fixed point where they will cross for sure.

133
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:23,140
On the other hand, what if one of them

134
00:05:23,140 --> 00:05:25,340
is skiing down the mountain, and another one

135
00:05:25,340 --> 00:05:27,380
is taking a funicular to the top?

136
00:05:27,380 --> 00:05:29,140
They might never cross.

137
00:05:29,140 --> 00:05:31,020
And again, that would be the case where there

138
00:05:31,020 --> 00:05:34,180
is an absence of a fixed point.

139
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:36,460
So that's the intuition behind what these fixed point

140
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:37,700
theorems are all about.

141
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:39,100
If you're curious, of course, you

142
00:05:39,100 --> 00:05:41,380
can go and read an enormous amount of math

143
00:05:41,380 --> 00:05:42,700
about it for general discussion.

144
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:43,820
We're not going to do that.

145
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:46,580
I'll just tell you that at the crucial moment,

146
00:05:46,580 --> 00:05:49,900
Gerard de Brul invoked the Brouwer's fixed point theorem.

147
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:52,140
And then there's another one that's even more general,

148
00:05:52,140 --> 00:05:54,580
called the Coccatani's fixed point theorem.

149
00:05:54,580 --> 00:05:56,580
If this is all more than you ever want to know,

150
00:05:56,580 --> 00:05:57,300
then well, good.

151
00:05:57,300 --> 00:05:58,860
I'm never going to tell you anymore.

152
00:05:58,860 --> 00:06:01,300
But if you want to learn more, on the other hand, bad news,

153
00:06:01,300 --> 00:06:04,660
because you'll have to pursue this on your own.

154
00:06:04,660 --> 00:06:08,220
OK, so questions about how this works?

155
00:06:08,220 --> 00:06:08,720
Yes?

156
00:06:08,720 --> 00:06:12,820
Unrelated, but sort of related, what do you mean by topology?

157
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:14,420
Topology is a branch of mathematics

158
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:17,460
concerned with things like fixed point theorems.

159
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:21,500
So again, you read the Wikipedia article on what topology is.

160
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:23,860
Again, honestly, the only topology that I know

161
00:06:23,860 --> 00:06:28,460
is the very topology that I had to learn for this very class.

162
00:06:28,460 --> 00:06:30,100
But basically, it's the mathematics

163
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:33,180
of shapes and spaces.

164
00:06:33,180 --> 00:06:35,500
Again, I couldn't give you any better description of it

165
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:38,940
than that, but there is a much more precise story

166
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:41,540
about what it is.

167
00:06:41,540 --> 00:06:44,660
Now, another question is, how do you get to these fixed points?

168
00:06:44,660 --> 00:06:46,820
So in general equilibrium theory,

169
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:49,980
they usually focus on Mooraisian auctioneers.

170
00:06:49,980 --> 00:06:52,580
This is the idea that there's just some person who

171
00:06:52,580 --> 00:06:54,940
looks around the world, and any time there's excess demand

172
00:06:54,940 --> 00:06:56,140
for something, he raises its price.

173
00:06:56,140 --> 00:06:58,440
And any time there's excess supply, he lowers its price.

174
00:06:58,440 --> 00:07:00,300
And he does this in a continuous way,

175
00:07:00,300 --> 00:07:02,540
using a very nice blue function.

176
00:07:02,540 --> 00:07:04,340
And in this way, the economy gets

177
00:07:04,340 --> 00:07:06,660
to the equilibrium price vector.

178
00:07:06,660 --> 00:07:08,780
There is a whole literature where Austrians talk

179
00:07:08,780 --> 00:07:13,100
about how terrible this is, and how awful general equilibrium

180
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:15,900
theory is for dodging this great point.

181
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:17,700
Because of course, is there actually

182
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:20,140
an auctioneer in the real world generally?

183
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:20,900
No.

184
00:07:20,900 --> 00:07:22,660
Sometimes in financial markets, there

185
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:24,660
will be a market maker who is actually

186
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:26,580
a lot like a Mooraisian auctioneer.

187
00:07:26,580 --> 00:07:28,060
But then in a lot of other markets,

188
00:07:28,060 --> 00:07:29,580
there's nobody like that.

189
00:07:29,580 --> 00:07:32,340
So this is a bit of a puzzle.

190
00:07:32,340 --> 00:07:34,180
We'll discuss some alternatives later on.

191
00:07:34,180 --> 00:07:35,540
Of course, the really obvious one

192
00:07:35,540 --> 00:07:39,060
is that usually, sellers just set their own price.

193
00:07:39,060 --> 00:07:41,260
And usually, we don't have perfect competition.

194
00:07:41,260 --> 00:07:43,020
So the fact that you set a different price

195
00:07:43,020 --> 00:07:45,180
for a competitor does not mean that you will either

196
00:07:45,180 --> 00:07:47,980
sell an infinite amount or zero.

197
00:07:47,980 --> 00:07:50,480
Like, have you ever noticed that you want to sell something

198
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:52,940
on, say, eBay, and then you charge a price a little bit

199
00:07:52,940 --> 00:07:56,860
higher than other people, and you still sell it?

200
00:07:56,860 --> 00:07:59,260
In perfect competition, that would never happen.

201
00:07:59,260 --> 00:08:00,740
Or have you ever noticed that you

202
00:08:00,740 --> 00:08:01,980
say I'm willing to work for a little bit less

203
00:08:01,980 --> 00:08:03,580
than other people, and then you don't

204
00:08:03,580 --> 00:08:07,420
find that you receive a trillion job offers?

205
00:08:07,420 --> 00:08:09,740
And again, that's not what perfect competition predicts.

206
00:08:09,740 --> 00:08:11,240
Perfect competition says that if you

207
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:14,860
offer to work for a penny less than equally qualified workers,

208
00:08:14,860 --> 00:08:18,100
everyone in the world will come and try to hire you.

209
00:08:18,100 --> 00:08:20,700
So far, I don't know of anyone, no matter how successful,

210
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:24,300
has been offered a job by everyone in the world.

211
00:08:24,300 --> 00:08:25,540
OK.

212
00:08:25,540 --> 00:08:28,140
All right, so questions about this?

213
00:08:28,140 --> 00:08:29,380
Happy?

214
00:08:29,380 --> 00:08:30,140
All right.

215
00:08:30,140 --> 00:08:31,300
So now we're going to do something

216
00:08:31,300 --> 00:08:33,020
which I think will actually enhance your understanding

217
00:08:33,020 --> 00:08:34,420
of what we just done.

218
00:08:34,420 --> 00:08:36,220
And this was never done for me.

219
00:08:36,220 --> 00:08:38,900
So I had to wait years before I actually achieved

220
00:08:38,900 --> 00:08:40,340
this higher level of insight.

221
00:08:40,340 --> 00:08:44,660
So when would a general equilibrium not exist?

222
00:08:44,660 --> 00:08:46,100
It's all well and good to go and say

223
00:08:46,100 --> 00:08:49,420
we proved the existence of general equilibrium,

224
00:08:49,420 --> 00:08:50,880
and we have a bunch of assumptions.

225
00:08:50,880 --> 00:08:53,180
But if you don't understand why the assumptions matter,

226
00:08:53,180 --> 00:08:55,700
then I would still say that you're pretty much in the dark

227
00:08:55,700 --> 00:08:57,740
and don't have any intuition for what's going on.

228
00:08:57,740 --> 00:09:01,700
So rather than going into great detail

229
00:09:01,700 --> 00:09:03,380
on what the fixed point theorems are,

230
00:09:03,380 --> 00:09:05,060
I'm going to go and try to explain to you

231
00:09:05,060 --> 00:09:08,900
why the different assumptions actually make a difference.

232
00:09:08,900 --> 00:09:12,980
So here's some counter examples where general equilibrium would

233
00:09:12,980 --> 00:09:14,660
not, in fact, exist.

234
00:09:14,660 --> 00:09:16,060
All right, first big one.

235
00:09:16,060 --> 00:09:17,780
Lexicographic preferences.

236
00:09:17,780 --> 00:09:20,300
So you guys remember hearing about lexicographic preferences

237
00:09:20,300 --> 00:09:21,420
at some point?

238
00:09:21,420 --> 00:09:24,740
So in the dictionary, when you are alphabetizing words,

239
00:09:24,740 --> 00:09:29,860
suppose that there is a word that is like AZZZZZZZZ.

240
00:09:29,860 --> 00:09:30,940
That's a word.

241
00:09:30,940 --> 00:09:33,740
All right, now does the fact that it has a bunch of Z's

242
00:09:33,740 --> 00:09:36,660
mean that it gets moved later in the dictionary

243
00:09:36,660 --> 00:09:42,300
than a word that has, that's like ABBA?

244
00:09:42,300 --> 00:09:44,500
All right, where the average letter in ABBA

245
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:47,900
is way closer to A than the average letter in A

246
00:09:47,900 --> 00:09:49,780
with 10 Z's on it, right?

247
00:09:49,780 --> 00:09:53,660
Now, in dictionaries, we use a lexicographic order

248
00:09:53,660 --> 00:09:55,740
where we first look at the first letter

249
00:09:55,740 --> 00:09:57,340
and organize everything that way.

250
00:09:57,340 --> 00:09:58,340
Then we look at the second letter

251
00:09:58,340 --> 00:09:59,740
and organize everything that way.

252
00:09:59,740 --> 00:10:01,420
Then we look at the third letter and organize everything

253
00:10:01,420 --> 00:10:02,700
that way, right?

254
00:10:02,700 --> 00:10:06,380
And this way, we proceed with a clear priority.

255
00:10:06,380 --> 00:10:08,260
First letter is absolute priority

256
00:10:08,260 --> 00:10:10,860
unless the first letter of two words is the same.

257
00:10:10,860 --> 00:10:12,300
Then we look at the second letter.

258
00:10:12,300 --> 00:10:13,220
If the second letter is the same,

259
00:10:13,220 --> 00:10:14,100
we look at the third letter.

260
00:10:14,100 --> 00:10:15,980
If the third letter is the same, we look at the fourth letter.

261
00:10:15,980 --> 00:10:18,220
That's the way that we alphabetize things.

262
00:10:18,220 --> 00:10:20,860
Imagine then that someone had preferences for consumption

263
00:10:20,860 --> 00:10:23,660
this way where they said, look, if there's two bundles

264
00:10:23,660 --> 00:10:26,620
and one has more X, I always choose the one with more X.

265
00:10:26,620 --> 00:10:28,460
If they have equal X, then I choose the one

266
00:10:28,460 --> 00:10:29,820
with it that has more Y.

267
00:10:29,820 --> 00:10:31,540
If they have equal X and equal Y,

268
00:10:31,540 --> 00:10:33,700
then I choose the one that has more C, and so on.

269
00:10:33,700 --> 00:10:36,620
That is what lexicographic preferences mean.

270
00:10:36,620 --> 00:10:40,580
All right, now, if someone has lexicographic preferences,

271
00:10:40,580 --> 00:10:42,740
then it is easy to prove, or maybe it

272
00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:45,940
can be proven anyway, that they cannot have any utility

273
00:10:45,940 --> 00:10:46,860
function.

274
00:10:46,860 --> 00:10:48,820
They do not have any kind of utility function.

275
00:10:48,820 --> 00:10:49,700
Why?

276
00:10:49,700 --> 00:10:52,620
Because if you have lexicographic preferences,

277
00:10:52,620 --> 00:10:54,900
there is no amount of Y that can compensate you

278
00:10:54,900 --> 00:10:57,700
for even the slightest amount of X.

279
00:10:57,700 --> 00:10:59,340
And that's the problem.

280
00:10:59,340 --> 00:11:02,860
OK, now, why does this mean that we could no longer be

281
00:11:02,860 --> 00:11:04,740
sure of the shadow equilibrium?

282
00:11:04,740 --> 00:11:09,740
Well, so suppose that all the agents of the economy

283
00:11:09,740 --> 00:11:11,580
had lexicographic preferences.

284
00:11:11,580 --> 00:11:14,420
Then, so we know, like, everyone lexicographically

285
00:11:14,420 --> 00:11:16,460
prefer X to Y.

286
00:11:16,460 --> 00:11:22,220
Well, is it then possible for X to have a price less than 1?

287
00:11:22,220 --> 00:11:25,220
No, because if the price were less than 1,

288
00:11:25,220 --> 00:11:30,260
what would every single person do in order to get more X?

289
00:11:30,260 --> 00:11:32,900
So if the price of X were, like, 0.999,

290
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:36,540
and the price of Y were 0.001, if you

291
00:11:36,540 --> 00:11:40,500
had lexicographic preferences, would you keep any Y?

292
00:11:40,500 --> 00:11:42,660
It's really cheap, no, but you dump all your Y

293
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:47,500
to buy X, because you value X, the smallest amount of X,

294
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:50,820
infinitely more than you value an infinite amount of Y.

295
00:11:50,820 --> 00:11:53,060
All right, so this means that you say, all right, fine.

296
00:11:53,060 --> 00:11:56,660
Then the equilibrium price is going to be the price of X is 1,

297
00:11:56,660 --> 00:11:58,020
the price of Y is 0.

298
00:11:58,020 --> 00:11:59,620
But that's not an equilibrium either,

299
00:11:59,620 --> 00:12:01,860
because we specified that if the amount of X is equal,

300
00:12:01,860 --> 00:12:04,620
people would still like as much Y as possible.

301
00:12:04,620 --> 00:12:07,140
So if the price of X is 1, then people

302
00:12:07,140 --> 00:12:11,060
want an infinite amount of Y. If the price of X is less than 1,

303
00:12:11,060 --> 00:12:14,740
people want 0 Y. And therefore, it

304
00:12:14,740 --> 00:12:17,700
is possible there would be no equilibrium at all.

305
00:12:17,700 --> 00:12:20,200
And that's an example of the failure of general equilibrium.

306
00:12:20,200 --> 00:12:23,260
There's no price where the world makes sense.

307
00:12:23,260 --> 00:12:25,260
And you say, all right, so the price of X is 1.

308
00:12:25,260 --> 00:12:26,660
Well, then it doesn't make sense,

309
00:12:26,660 --> 00:12:29,660
because people demand an infinite amount of Y,

310
00:12:29,660 --> 00:12:31,500
and there isn't an infinite amount of Y.

311
00:12:31,500 --> 00:12:34,220
So fine, let's make the price of X 0.999.

312
00:12:34,220 --> 00:12:36,380
Well, that's not going to make any sense either,

313
00:12:36,380 --> 00:12:41,180
because now people want 0 Y, and there's more than 0 Y.

314
00:12:41,180 --> 00:12:44,020
So no equilibrium.

315
00:12:44,020 --> 00:12:47,500
The economy does not make sense in this situation.

316
00:12:47,500 --> 00:12:48,620
All right, another example.

317
00:12:48,620 --> 00:12:50,900
Suppose there's a discontinuity of any kind.

318
00:12:50,900 --> 00:12:52,220
So suppose the total demand for X

319
00:12:52,220 --> 00:12:56,820
is 90% of the social endowment at the price of 7,

320
00:12:56,820 --> 00:13:00,620
and 10% of the endowment at price of 7 minus epsilon,

321
00:13:00,620 --> 00:13:02,380
infinitesimal amount.

322
00:13:02,380 --> 00:13:03,900
So why would this be?

323
00:13:03,900 --> 00:13:05,460
Well, maybe society is a superstition

324
00:13:05,460 --> 00:13:08,740
centering on the number 0.7.

325
00:13:08,740 --> 00:13:10,660
All right, have you ever been in a building

326
00:13:10,660 --> 00:13:13,100
where there's no floor 13?

327
00:13:13,100 --> 00:13:14,900
All right, so that's weird.

328
00:13:14,900 --> 00:13:16,660
So I was on a cruise ship.

329
00:13:16,660 --> 00:13:18,100
There's no floor 13.

330
00:13:18,100 --> 00:13:19,980
It's not like it's a laundry floor.

331
00:13:19,980 --> 00:13:21,540
It just doesn't exist.

332
00:13:21,540 --> 00:13:24,420
You go on the stairs, and twilight's on.

333
00:13:24,420 --> 00:13:26,740
You go from 14 to 12.

334
00:13:26,740 --> 00:13:28,700
Where'd 13 go?

335
00:13:28,700 --> 00:13:30,060
All right, same idea.

336
00:13:30,060 --> 00:13:32,620
If there was a superstition about the number 0.7,

337
00:13:32,620 --> 00:13:35,340
so that could lead the people in a discontinuous response.

338
00:13:35,340 --> 00:13:37,180
And then again, no general equilibrium.

339
00:13:37,180 --> 00:13:38,420
There's a question over there.

340
00:13:38,420 --> 00:13:39,460
Yes, please.

341
00:13:39,460 --> 00:13:40,920
Can you have a general equilibrium

342
00:13:40,920 --> 00:13:43,220
with the on-chain preferences?

343
00:13:43,220 --> 00:13:44,300
Ah, so let's see.

344
00:13:44,300 --> 00:13:46,340
With the on-chain preferences, let's see.

345
00:13:46,340 --> 00:13:48,020
So let's see.

346
00:13:48,020 --> 00:13:49,620
So again, the on-chain preferences,

347
00:13:49,620 --> 00:13:51,300
that's actually an example.

348
00:13:51,300 --> 00:13:52,460
So let's see.

349
00:13:52,460 --> 00:13:54,740
So that's one where, let's see.

350
00:13:54,740 --> 00:14:01,300
So you would put 0 value on an additional amount of a good,

351
00:14:01,300 --> 00:14:03,300
unless it came packaged with an additional amount

352
00:14:03,300 --> 00:14:04,100
of the other good.

353
00:14:04,100 --> 00:14:06,860
So yes, I think we actually specifically ruled that out.

354
00:14:06,860 --> 00:14:07,380
Let's see.

355
00:14:07,380 --> 00:14:07,880
Yes, yes.

356
00:14:07,880 --> 00:14:12,380
So that would violate that assumption 4 in section 3,

357
00:14:12,380 --> 00:14:15,620
the utility strictly increasing in all commodities.

358
00:14:15,620 --> 00:14:18,300
Now, could a general equilibrium still exist?

359
00:14:18,300 --> 00:14:20,700
The answer is almost certainly absolutely.

360
00:14:20,700 --> 00:14:25,180
But we no longer have absolute proof that it will exist.

361
00:14:25,180 --> 00:14:28,060
And that is, again, the issue mathematically.

362
00:14:28,060 --> 00:14:30,460
So again, all of these are about sufficient conditions.

363
00:14:30,460 --> 00:14:33,940
So essentially, when normally, when any assumptions fail,

364
00:14:33,940 --> 00:14:36,220
you could just go and tweak all the other assumptions

365
00:14:36,220 --> 00:14:39,380
sufficiently so that everything works out nicely.

366
00:14:39,380 --> 00:14:41,900
Not always, but often you can.

367
00:14:41,900 --> 00:14:45,460
So yes, so with the likes of graphic preferences,

368
00:14:45,460 --> 00:14:47,540
here's a nice tweak that'll make the world work.

369
00:14:47,540 --> 00:14:50,500
Let's just assume there's no y.

370
00:14:50,500 --> 00:14:52,460
Then we've got an equilibrium.

371
00:14:52,460 --> 00:14:53,340
The price of x is 1.

372
00:14:53,340 --> 00:14:54,540
Price of y is 0.

373
00:14:54,540 --> 00:14:55,980
Nobody has any y.

374
00:14:55,980 --> 00:14:57,420
So everyone consumes their diamonds.

375
00:14:57,420 --> 00:14:59,020
Everything's great.

376
00:14:59,020 --> 00:15:01,300
So that'll be fine.

377
00:15:01,300 --> 00:15:02,420
All right.

378
00:15:02,420 --> 00:15:02,940
Let's see.

379
00:15:02,940 --> 00:15:04,820
Then there's a more general concept of demand

380
00:15:04,820 --> 00:15:06,740
not being well-behaved at extreme prices.

381
00:15:06,740 --> 00:15:09,320
This could mean that you just have a 0 price for 0 prices

382
00:15:09,320 --> 00:15:10,180
for some goods.

383
00:15:10,180 --> 00:15:13,820
But there's some technical issues I won't get into.

384
00:15:13,820 --> 00:15:14,700
Here's another one.

385
00:15:14,700 --> 00:15:17,180
Suppose that prices themselves are discontinuous.

386
00:15:17,180 --> 00:15:20,660
Suppose that prices must be in one penny units.

387
00:15:20,660 --> 00:15:25,780
Then it could be that the actual market clearing price is, say,

388
00:15:25,780 --> 00:15:31,620
$1.99 and 7 tenths of a cent.

389
00:15:31,620 --> 00:15:33,660
And then either the price is too high or too low.

390
00:15:33,660 --> 00:15:35,860
And in either case, you actually get market clearing.

391
00:15:35,860 --> 00:15:37,580
And there's no general equilibrium.

392
00:15:37,580 --> 00:15:40,540
So you have that problem.

393
00:15:40,540 --> 00:15:42,980
Now, as I said, these standard theorems

394
00:15:42,980 --> 00:15:44,820
give sufficient conditions.

395
00:15:44,820 --> 00:15:48,060
You can still get general equilibrium

396
00:15:48,060 --> 00:15:50,340
even if some of these assumptions are violated.

397
00:15:50,340 --> 00:15:52,060
So here's a fun one.

398
00:15:52,060 --> 00:15:54,500
Suppose you have a linear utility function.

399
00:15:54,500 --> 00:15:55,340
Many of you remember.

400
00:15:55,340 --> 00:15:57,100
Anyone remember back in intermediate micro?

401
00:15:57,100 --> 00:15:58,940
And you're like, why do we have to do utility functions

402
00:15:58,940 --> 00:15:59,820
like that one?

403
00:15:59,820 --> 00:16:02,900
Why can't it just be utilities x plus y?

404
00:16:02,900 --> 00:16:07,220
Why are you torturing us with these low utility functions?

405
00:16:07,220 --> 00:16:12,900
And the answer is that if your utility is equal to x plus y,

406
00:16:12,900 --> 00:16:16,140
then what does everybody want to do?

407
00:16:16,140 --> 00:16:18,740
So if your utility is x plus y, then you

408
00:16:18,740 --> 00:16:21,900
either want to have all x or all y,

409
00:16:21,900 --> 00:16:23,860
except in one special condition.

410
00:16:23,860 --> 00:16:28,300
So what prices would make a person with utility

411
00:16:28,300 --> 00:16:30,860
equals x plus y want to have all x?

412
00:16:30,860 --> 00:16:31,900
px is less than py.

413
00:16:31,900 --> 00:16:33,500
Yeah, px is less than py.

414
00:16:33,500 --> 00:16:34,860
Then dump all your y.

415
00:16:34,860 --> 00:16:35,820
Buy all x.

416
00:16:35,820 --> 00:16:36,940
Utility's higher.

417
00:16:36,940 --> 00:16:38,380
On the other hand, reverse it.

418
00:16:38,380 --> 00:16:40,980
So if x is more expensive than y, then dump all your x.

419
00:16:40,980 --> 00:16:42,020
Buy y.

420
00:16:42,020 --> 00:16:45,340
If the prices are the same, then you're OK.

421
00:16:45,340 --> 00:16:46,460
Prices of x are the same.

422
00:16:46,460 --> 00:16:50,300
Then you're indifferent between any bundle.

423
00:16:50,300 --> 00:16:53,460
So if it turned out that, say, everyone

424
00:16:53,460 --> 00:16:55,740
had a utility function equal to price of x

425
00:16:55,740 --> 00:16:58,100
plus the price of y, then you might still

426
00:16:58,100 --> 00:17:00,180
be able to get equilibrium.

427
00:17:00,180 --> 00:17:05,020
So if the prices are equal, then maybe everyone

428
00:17:05,020 --> 00:17:07,460
is just content to stay with their initial endowment.

429
00:17:07,460 --> 00:17:08,460
Why not?

430
00:17:08,460 --> 00:17:10,220
And then you're fine.

431
00:17:10,220 --> 00:17:12,260
Now, notice, if you think about it more,

432
00:17:12,260 --> 00:17:14,580
what about a world where almost everybody has a utility

433
00:17:14,580 --> 00:17:16,220
function like that, and then some people

434
00:17:16,220 --> 00:17:17,460
have these weird linear ones?

435
00:17:17,460 --> 00:17:19,660
Is that going to be OK?

436
00:17:19,660 --> 00:17:22,980
Almost certainly fine, because what happens?

437
00:17:22,980 --> 00:17:26,220
So 99% of people have those nice ones,

438
00:17:26,220 --> 00:17:28,380
and a log utility function.

439
00:17:28,380 --> 00:17:30,540
Then 1% of people have the linear ones.

440
00:17:30,540 --> 00:17:32,820
Well, if the price of x is greater than the price of y,

441
00:17:32,820 --> 00:17:35,420
what do the people with the linear functions do?

442
00:17:35,420 --> 00:17:37,180
Price of x is greater than the price of y.

443
00:17:37,180 --> 00:17:38,740
What do they all do?

444
00:17:38,740 --> 00:17:39,940
Dump all their x.

445
00:17:39,940 --> 00:17:41,620
And who buys it?

446
00:17:41,620 --> 00:17:42,580
Those people.

447
00:17:42,580 --> 00:17:44,420
And then the price adjusts, and those people

448
00:17:44,420 --> 00:17:46,380
go and adjust things.

449
00:17:46,380 --> 00:17:48,140
But again, this means that we can no longer

450
00:17:48,140 --> 00:17:53,020
have that pure mathematical Pythagorean certainty

451
00:17:53,020 --> 00:17:55,620
that mathematicians are hunting for, because they say,

452
00:17:55,620 --> 00:17:56,980
well, that's pretty good.

453
00:17:56,980 --> 00:18:00,540
So if there's 99% that will work, but 1% that is OK,

454
00:18:00,540 --> 00:18:02,540
but what if it's 47%?

455
00:18:02,540 --> 00:18:04,380
What if it's radical 3?

456
00:18:04,380 --> 00:18:05,380
What if it's that?

457
00:18:05,380 --> 00:18:07,260
You don't have any proof of anything, do you?

458
00:18:07,260 --> 00:18:11,780
It's all just loosey-goosey garbage, complete garbage.

459
00:18:11,780 --> 00:18:14,500
Very like, well, not a problem that's

460
00:18:14,500 --> 00:18:16,220
really that pressing anyway.

461
00:18:16,220 --> 00:18:18,380
So you could go and work it as a homework problem,

462
00:18:18,380 --> 00:18:19,180
and then you could say, well, we've

463
00:18:19,180 --> 00:18:20,940
proven it for this homework problem.

464
00:18:20,940 --> 00:18:22,780
But again, if you are a mathematical economist saying,

465
00:18:22,780 --> 00:18:24,380
bah, you proved it for one homework problem,

466
00:18:24,380 --> 00:18:26,700
that's not good enough to get a dream job for life.

467
00:18:26,700 --> 00:18:29,340
I want a very general proof of the conditions,

468
00:18:29,340 --> 00:18:31,620
defining the entire locus of points

469
00:18:31,620 --> 00:18:33,700
under which general equilibrium will still exist.

470
00:18:33,700 --> 00:18:36,260
If a fraction of population A has linear preferences,

471
00:18:36,260 --> 00:18:38,780
and a fraction of B has lower linear preferences,

472
00:18:38,780 --> 00:18:39,620
let's do that.

473
00:18:39,620 --> 00:18:41,340
And then you can get a paper out of that,

474
00:18:41,340 --> 00:18:42,740
and probably somebody has done it.

475
00:18:46,740 --> 00:18:50,140
So anyway, so yes, so with this linear utility function,

476
00:18:50,140 --> 00:18:53,500
we have a problem where we have discontinuous responses

477
00:18:53,500 --> 00:18:55,180
to demand to price.

478
00:18:55,180 --> 00:18:59,060
Because either you consume all x, or you're different,

479
00:18:59,060 --> 00:18:59,860
or all y.

480
00:18:59,860 --> 00:19:01,860
And if you're graphing that, you would see, wow,

481
00:19:01,860 --> 00:19:03,260
that's a big discontinuity.

482
00:19:03,260 --> 00:19:08,220
Either all of x, or all of y, or you don't care.

483
00:19:08,220 --> 00:19:10,980
And we can do similar things for most of these assumptions.

484
00:19:10,980 --> 00:19:12,660
We'll say, well, what can we also do?

485
00:19:12,660 --> 00:19:14,300
How might it still work out?

486
00:19:14,300 --> 00:19:17,580
Again, imagine a world where everybody

487
00:19:17,580 --> 00:19:19,340
has one good they don't care about.

488
00:19:19,340 --> 00:19:21,980
Intuitively, what's going to happen in that world?

489
00:19:21,980 --> 00:19:24,300
If someone happens to start with an endowment of a good

490
00:19:24,300 --> 00:19:28,740
they don't like at all, what are you going to do?

491
00:19:28,740 --> 00:19:29,580
Sell it.

492
00:19:29,580 --> 00:19:31,820
But since there's always somebody who likes each good,

493
00:19:31,820 --> 00:19:34,700
then it'll just drive down the price of that a little bit.

494
00:19:34,700 --> 00:19:38,420
And the people who do like it will take it and the problem.

495
00:19:38,420 --> 00:19:40,060
OK, everyone happy?

496
00:19:40,060 --> 00:19:41,900
Questions, questions?

497
00:19:41,900 --> 00:19:44,740
All right, so certainly this stage of the evening,

498
00:19:44,740 --> 00:19:47,260
I'm always worried lack of questions simply means lack

499
00:19:47,260 --> 00:19:48,700
of energy to put your hand up.

500
00:19:48,700 --> 00:19:50,940
Or it's just like, fine, I'll take your word for it.

501
00:19:50,940 --> 00:19:56,740
But let's try to steel ourselves in pursuit of PhD level

